<h3>Set Up Storm Tracking and Alert Systems</h3>

<p>
You cannot respond to storms you do not know about. Set up multiple monitoring systems to catch events as they happen:
</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>National Weather Service alerts:</strong> Subscribe to severe weather alerts for every market you serve. The NWS issues severe thunderstorm warnings, tornado warnings, and hail alerts in real time.</li>
<li><strong>Hail tracking apps:</strong> Tools like HailStrike, Interactive Hail Maps, and WeatherOps provide precise hail swath mapping. You can see exactly which neighborhoods received 1-inch, 1.5-inch, or 2-inch hail.</li>
<li><strong>NOAA Storm Events Database:</strong> Historical data on every reported severe weather event. Use this to identify your market's storm frequency and plan staffing.</li>
<li><strong>Social media monitoring:</strong> Set up Google Alerts and social listening for "hail [your city]" and "storm damage [your city]." Homeowners post photos within minutes of an event.</li>
</ul>

<p>
HailStrike and similar tools cost $200 to $500 per month but pay for themselves with a single accurate storm identification. A contractor who knows about a hail storm in a specific zip code within two hours can deploy door-knockers and launch geo-targeted ads before competitors even check the weather.
</p>

<h3>Pre-Position Content and Ad Creative</h3>

<p>
Write your storm content before the season starts. When a storm hits, you need to publish immediately, not draft blog posts. Prepare the following content library:
</p>

<ul>
<li>5 to 10 blog posts covering different storm types and damage signs</li>
<li>3 to 5 video scripts for emergency response content</li>
<li>20 to 30 social media posts with storm damage photo templates</li>
<li>Email sequences for storm-affected homeowner lists</li>
<li>Ad copy variations for Google, Facebook, and Nextdoor</li>
</ul>

<p>
For ad creative, prepare image templates with your branding that you can populate with storm-specific photos within an hour. Canva or Figma templates work well. Include your phone number, "Free Inspection," and "We Bill Insurance" on every image.
</p>

<h3>Train Your Team for Storm Response</h3>

<p>
Storm mode is different from normal operations. Everyone on your team needs to know their role before the event. Create a storm response playbook that covers:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Who monitors weather alerts and makes the call to activate storm mode</li>
<li>How to adjust ad budgets and activate pre-built campaigns</li>
<li>Who answers phones and how to handle the surge in calls</li>
<li>Inspection scheduling protocol for same-day and next-day appointments</li>
<li>Crew dispatch procedures for emergency tarping and temporary repairs</li>
<li>Documentation requirements for insurance claims</li>
<li>Communication with adjusters and insurance companies</li>
</ul>

<p>
Run a tabletop drill before storm season. Simulate a hail storm hitting your primary service area. Walk through every step from alert to first inspection. Identify bottlenecks and fix them before real money is on the line.
</p>

